============================================================================
2. the lambda-deficit form is the weakest of the three
============================================================================
  lambda = log_rho 32 = 5 alpha = 14.71237298.
  The transfer floor is, exactly up to the additive 73/17,
    17 r_min(t+B_t) + 73 >= (17*2^{5t+1}+73) rho^{-B_t},
  and 2^{5t} = rho^{lambda t}, so the floor is 2 rho^{lambda t-B_t}
  up to fixed constants.  Verified as an exact rational identity:
    t=12   b=6   : floor 10^17.749     2 rho^{lambda t-b} = 10^17.749
    t=40   b=1   : floor 10^60.405     2 rho^{lambda t-b} = 10^60.405
    t=48   b=1   : floor 10^72.446     2 rho^{lambda t-b} = 10^72.446
    t=100  b=14  : floor 10^149.384    2 rho^{lambda t-b} = 10^149.384
  So the sufficient conditions, from weakest to strongest:
    (i)   lambda t - B_t -> +infinity along SOME sequence
          (even a log log t deficit), giving an unbounded floor;
    (ii)  B_t <= 14t at arbitrarily large t: deficit >= 0.712 t,
          so an EXPONENTIAL floor 2 * 1.182713^t;
    (iii) liminf (1/T) sum B_t < infinity: infinitely many t with
          B_t = O(1), so a floor of order 32^t at depth t + O(1).
  (iii) => (ii) => (i).  I called (iii) the weakest sufficient
  condition in the programme; (i) is weaker, and is the right
  statement of the target.  Corrected.
    a log log t deficit at t = 10^3 gives only a floor of about 1.58
    a log log t deficit at t = 10^6 gives only a floor of about 1.86
  -- so (i) is genuinely weak: the floor it produces may grow
  arbitrarily slowly.  Monotonicity of r_min is what makes that
  enough.

============================================================================
3. the 2^{ceil((5t-2)/6)} ceiling is SHARP: their family confirms it
============================================================================
  Comparison family  x_eps = 7 + 8 sum eps_i 64^i,  eps in {0,1}^t.
  It shares three structural facts with the real residue set:
    the two admissible residues 7 and 15 mod 64,
    the nesting (a prefix fixes x mod 64^k),
    the first-difference valuations v_2 = 3 + 6i.
    t=3   K=3   count = 2^K = 8      (of 2^3)
    t=4   K=3   count = 2^K = 8      (of 2^4)
    t=5   K=4   count = 2^K = 16     (of 2^5)
    t=6   K=5   count = 2^K = 32     (of 2^6)
    ... exact agreement for every t = 3..14, rounding included.
  Reason: 8*64^i = 2^{3+6i}, every term with 3+6i <= 5t fits (their
  total is < 2^{5t}*64/63 < 2^{5t+1}) and any single term with
  3+6i >= 5t+1 overshoots, so the count is
    2^{#{i : 3+6i <= 5t}} = 2^{floor((5t-3)/6)+1} = 2^{ceil((5t-2)/6)}
  using floor((n-1)/6)+1 = ceil(n/6), checked for t = 3..59.
  CONSEQUENCE.  No argument using only those three facts can beat
  2^{5t/6}.  My bound is the exact ceiling of the structure-only
  axis, and any improvement must use arithmetic specific to the
  block maps.  This CLOSES the 2-adic axis for B_t.

