============================================================================
5. where the intersection is hard: v_u is a middle-bit window
============================================================================
  From 64^{5m} v_u = 81^{5m} r_u + C_u, the quantity that decides
  a match is
    v_u mod 64^m = floor((81^{5m} r_u + C_u)/2^{30m}) mod 2^{6m},
  i.e. BITS 30m..36m OF THE PRODUCT 81^{5m} r_u.  The low 30m bits
  of that product are what pin r_u (they encode the prefix), and
  the top bits are what the window cutoff controls.  The matching
  condition reads the bits in between.
    m=1: verified on 40 prefixes.
    m=2: verified on 40 prefixes.
    m=3: verified on 40 prefixes.
  This is a 'middle bits of a multiplication' statement: the 2-adic
  side fixes the low window and the archimedean side fixes the top,
  and the matching bits are exactly the ones neither controls.  It
  is the same 2-adic/archimedean decoupling that capped every
  earlier axis, now in its sharpest form -- and it is why part 3's
  structure-only ceiling cannot be improved without new arithmetic.

  DIAGNOSTIC: is S_m special?  Count matches against random sets
  of the same size, to see whether the real survivor set behaves
  atypically.  (Evidence only; proves nothing.)
    m=2: real 2, random mean 1.070 (reference 1.0), P(random >= real) = 0.302
    m=3: real 3, random mean 0.963 (reference 1.0), P(random >= real) = 0.077
  So the real survivor set looks typical at these depths: the
  counts are consistent with Poisson(1), which is exactly why no
  conspiracy is visible to exploit and no bound follows.

